  "query_preview": "..\nKey Message 1)\nTemperatures are at their warmest to start the day as the cold front\napproaches from the west early this morning. As the cold front moves\nthrough between 6 AM and 1 PM, temperatures in the upper 60s to \nlower 70s quickly drop into the 50s, remaining there through the \nafternoon. A period of gusty winds between 25 and 35 mph will occur \nwith the frontal passage and through the afternoon, especially across\nwest-central and central Ohio. \n\nA very thin line of showers may eventually develop along the front as\nit moves through central/southern Ohio and northeast Kentucky late\nthis morning and into the early afternoon, however, much of the\nrainfall in the forecast is associated with favorable jet streak\ndynamics with the deep trough moving through the region. The period\nof light rain develops aloft behind the front, persisting through \nthe afternoon. While light, this rainfall will only add to the dreary\nconditions behind the front. \n\nThe better jet dynamics shift off to the east during the early\nevening hours, but the main trough axis will begin to pivot \nsoutheast through the Great Lakes and into the Ohio Valley this \nevening. The trough drives a secondary cold front southward through \nthe area this evening, bringing a renewed period of wind gusts \nbetween 25 and 35 mph for tonight. Temperatures will be driven down \nfurther, pushing low temperatures into the upper 30s to lower 40s. \nGiven the expectation that the winds will not go calm, frost \npotential is lower for tonight into Saturday morning. \n\nKey Message 2) \nThe trough shifts to the east on Saturday, allowing high pressure to\nbuild directly over the Ohio Valley by Saturday evening. Clouds are\nlikely a bit stubborn Saturday afternoon across central/southern\nOhio, but they eventually clear by the evening hours. As noted in \nprevious forecasts, given the placement of the high pressure, the \ncurrent forecast for low temperatures Saturday night is a couple \ndegrees lower than NBM deterministic guidance. Most areas drop into \nthe lower 30s with upper 20s also occurring, especially within the \nvalleys. A freeze watch will be needed at some point, within the \nnext one or two forecast cycles. \n\nThe center of the high pressure shifts off to the east on Sunday, but\nconditions will remain favorable for frost development into Monday\nmorning. \n\nTemperatures gradually moderate each day during the next work week.\nThey are back to seasonal normals on Monday, rising a few degrees \nabove normal Tuesday through Friday. Depending on the evolution of \nthe cut-off or closed-low off the East Coast, rainfall projections \nthrough all of next week are fairly dry, with below normal PWATs \npersisting over the region.",
